        "CLOCK-1"
      ],
      "severity": "minor",
      "status": "resolved",
      "descriptionMarkdown": "**Fling rest offset is asserted bit-exactly on a libm-dependent value.**\n\n- **Expected:** lane A runs `deterministic_motion_clock` on Windows, Linux and macOS, so its cross-OS assertions hold on all three.\n- **Observed:** `touch_fling_decays_to_a_deterministic_rest_offset` asserts `resting == -699.387_76` with exact `f32` equality. That value was recorded on Windows. The default `GestureTuning` uses `ScrollPhysics::ios()` (`Exponential`), whose `fling_duration`/`fling_distance` call `f32::ln` and `f32::powf`. Rust documents both as platform-dependent in precision, so UCRT, glibc and Apple libm can differ by an ULP. At about 700 px one f32 ULP is about 6e-5, which breaks the exact literal. The text group in the same sprint already avoids this with a 1e-3 tolerance (`assert_px`).\n- **Repair:** assert the absolute rest offset within a small tolerance, for example `(resting - (-699.38776)).abs() < 1e-3`. Keep exact equality for `released == -80` (pure arithmetic) and for the same-seed comparison in `identical_steps_with_the_same_seed_give_identical_fling_offsets` (same binary, same machine).\n\n**Resolved:** the rest offset is now compared to -699.387_76 within 1e-3 px, with a comment on platform-dependent `ln`/`powf` precision. `released == -80` and the same-seed comparison stay exact.",
      "regressionRefs": [
        {
          "repositoryId": "gpux",
          "path": "crates/gpui/tests/deterministic_motion_clock.rs",
          "symbol": "touch_fling_decays_to_a_deterministic_rest_offset"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S1-F2",
      "boundaryId": "s1-test-platform-foundations",
      "criterionIds": [
        "CLOCK-1"
      ],
      "severity": "minor",
      "status": "resolved",
      "descriptionMarkdown": "**The new frame-stepping helpers' docs omit inactive-window throttling.**\n\n- **Expected:** `VisualTestContext::simulate_frame`/`advance_frame` and `TestAppWindow::simulate_frame`/`advance_frame` are the advertised way to step clock-driven motion. Their contract should say when a frame does not run.\n- **Observed:** the docs say each call \"runs the callbacks scheduled with `Window::on_next_frame` and draws when the window is dirty\". But:\n  - `TestWindow::is_active()` is false, so test windows open inactive.\n  - The frame throttle in `window.rs` now reads the executor clock and caps inactive windows with pending frame callbacks to `inactive_frame_interval` (33 ms).\n  - So `advance_frame(16ms)` on a default test window silently skips every other frame.\n  \n  The new motion group works around this with a private `activate()` helper; the API docs never say so.\n- **Repair:** document the behaviour in all four helpers: test windows open inactive, and inactive windows throttle frame callbacks to `WindowOptions::inactive_frame_interval` on the executor clock. Tell callers to activate the window (`window.activate_window()`) or to step at least that interval. Optionally add one test that pins this contract: an inactive window skips a 16 ms frame, an active one does not.\n\n**Resolved:** all four helpers now document the inactive-window throttle and the two ways around it (activate the window, or step at least the interval). The new test `inactive_windows_skip_frames_inside_their_inactive_interval` pins the contract: a 16 ms frame is skipped while inactive, the pending frame arrives after the interval, and every frame is delivered once active.",
      "regressionRefs": [
        {
          "repositoryId": "gpux",
          "path": "crates/gpui/src/app/test_context.rs",
          "symbol": "VisualTestContext::simulate_frame"
        },
