    {
      "id": "S2-F2",
      "criterionIds": [
        "HEADLESS-3"
      ],
      "severity": "minor",
      "status": "resolved",
      "descriptionMarkdown": "**No test runs the unsupported-effect fallback on Windows or Linux.**\n\n**Expected:** HEADLESS-3 needs headless tests showing two things: effect frames render when the renderer reports support, and they take their documented fallback when it does not.\n\n**Observed:** `effect_frames_render_when_the_renderer_supports_them` (`crates/gpui/tests/headless_screenshot_context.rs:171`) branches on the reported capability. On Windows and Linux the wgpu renderer always reports support, so the fallback branch (line 202) runs only on macOS Metal. This sprint cannot run that host, and its CI lane is not green yet. The unsupported half of the criterion therefore has no test that has actually run.\n\n**Repair:** add a test to the same group that builds a context with `TestAppContext::build_with_renderer`. Its factory wraps `gpui_platform::current_headless_renderer()` in a test renderer that delegates `render_scene_to_image`, `render_scene` and `sprite_atlas` but keeps the default `effect_capabilities()`. Assert that the window reports no effect capabilities and that the `layer_blur` capture equals the sharp capture. The fallback then runs on every host.\n\n**Resolved (2de8686):** the new test wraps `current_headless_renderer()` in a renderer that keeps the default (empty) capabilities and skips effect batches. Metal does the same (`PrimitiveBatch::VisualEffects(_) => true`). Delegating alone would not be enough, because GPUI emits effect primitives whatever the capabilities say and the renderer decides whether to draw them.\n\nThe test asserts that the window reports no capabilities, that GPUI emitted exactly one effect primitive (zero without the blur), and that the blurred capture equals the sharp capture pixel for pixel. It runs on every host (6/6 on Windows).",
      "regressionRefs": [
